{
  "dom_id": "step-q254912",
  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Suppose $a=[1,\\,-2]^{T}$ and $b=[-7,\\,8]^{T}.$ Find the Chebyshev distance between $a$ and $b.$\n\n${d}_{\\infty }(a,b)=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that the Chebyshev distance is defined as\n\n$$\n{d}_{\\infty }(a,b)=‖a-b{‖}_{\\infty }\n$$\n\nwhere for an $N$-dimensional vector $v,$\n\n$$\n‖v{‖}_{\\infty }=max(|{v}_{1}|,|{v}_{2}|,…,|{v}_{N}|).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{d}_{\\infty }(a,b) & =‖a-b{‖}_{\\infty } \\\\  & ={‖[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]‖}_{\\infty } \\\\  & =max(|1-(-7)|,|-2-8|) \\\\  & =max(8,10) \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
